How many ways (solutions) are there to access instance data when static is involved?
👉 There are EXACTLY 3 standard & accepted ways.
(These are also what interviewers expect.)

✅ SOLUTION 1: Pass object to static method ⭐ (MOST COMMON)
Code
class Assignment2_2 {
    int a = 20;
    int b = 30;

    static void add(Assignment2_2 obj) {
        System.out.println(obj.a + obj.b);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Assignment2_2 obj = new Assignment2_2();
        add(obj);
    }
}

Why use this?

✔️ Clean
✔️ Efficient
✔️ Best for static utility methods

🎤 Interview line:

We pass the object reference to access instance variables inside a static method.

✅ SOLUTION 2: Make variables static (CLASS-LEVEL DATA)
Code
class Assignment2_2 {
    static int a = 20;
    static int b = 30;

    static void add() {
        System.out.println(a + b);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Assignment2_2.add();
    }
}

When to use?

✔️ When data is common for all objects

❌ Don’t use if each object needs different values

🎤 Interview line:

Static variables belong to the class, so static methods can access them directly.

✅ SOLUTION 3: Make method NON-static (PURE OOP WAY ⭐)
Code
class Assignment2_2 {
    int a = 20;
    int b = 30;

    void add() {
        System.out.println(a + b);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Assignment2_2 obj = new Assignment2_2();
        obj.add();
    }
}

Why this is best in real projects?

✔️ Proper OOP
✔️ No confusion
✔️ Most used in industry

🎤 Interview line:

Instance methods can directly access instance variables using object reference.

