When instance data is in a DIFFERENT CLASS, how many ways are there to access it?

This is a VERY important interview topic.
Let’s lock it in cleanly.

✅ WHEN DATA IS IN A DIFFERENT CLASS

👉 There are EXACTLY 4 STANDARD WAYS

I’ll show simple code + when to use.

🟢 CLASS 1 (Data class)
class Demo {
    int a = 10;
    int b = 20;
}

✅ WAY 1: Create object inside method (SIMPLE, NOT OPTIMAL)
class Test {
    void add() {
        Demo d = new Demo();
        System.out.println(d.a + d.b);
    }
}


✔️ Easy
❌ Creates new object every time
❌ Not recommended for performance

🎤 Interview note:

This approach is simple but not memory-efficient.

✅ WAY 2: Pass object as parameter ⭐ (BEST PRACTICE)
class Test {
    void add(Demo d) {
        System.out.println(d.a + d.b);
    }
}

Demo d = new Demo();
Test t = new Test();
t.add(d);


✔️ One object
✔️ Reusable
✔️ Industry standard

🎤 Interview line:

Object is created once and passed wherever required.

✅ WAY 3: Constructor Injection ⭐⭐ (VERY IMPORTANT)
class Test {
    Demo d;

    Test(Demo d) {
        this.d = d;
    }

    void add() {
        System.out.println(d.a + d.b);
    }
}

Demo d = new Demo();
Test t = new Test(d);
t.add();


✔️ Clean design
✔️ Loose coupling
✔️ Used in Spring / real projects

🎤 Interview line:

Dependency is provided through constructor.

✅ WAY 4: Make data static (CLASS-LEVEL SHARING)
class Demo {
    static int a = 10;
    static int b = 20;
}

class Test {
    void add() {
        System.out.println(Demo.a + Demo.b);
    }
}


✔️ No object needed
❌ Not object-oriented
❌ Shared by all objects

🎤 Interview warning:

Use static only if data is common for all objects.

❌ NOT RECOMMENDED / WRONG THINKING

Accessing instance variables directly without object ❌

Creating objects repeatedly inside methods ❌

