Java Variables — Cases with Examples
Case 1: Declare instance variable at class level

✅ Allowed

class Test {
    int x;   // instance variable
}


Why:
Instance variables define the state of an object.

Case 2: Initialize instance variable at class level

✅ Allowed

class Test {
    int x = 10;
}


Why:
Initialization at declaration happens when the object is created.

Case 3: Declare instance variable inside instance method

❌ Not allowed

class Test {
    void show() {
        int x;   // ❌ this is a local variable, not instance variable
    }
}


Why:
Variables inside methods are local, not part of the object.

Case 4: Initialize instance variable inside instance method

✅ Allowed

class Test {
    int x;

    void setX() {
        x = 20;   // ✔ assigning instance variable
    }
}


Why:
The method runs on an object and can modify its data.

Case 5: Declare static variable at class level

✅ Allowed

class Test {
    static int y;
}


Why:
Static variables belong to the class, not to objects.

Case 6: Initialize static variable at class level

✅ Allowed

class Test {
    static int y = 30;
}

Case 7: Declare static variable inside static method

❌ Not allowed

class Test {
    static void show() {
        static int y = 10;  // ❌ compile-time error
    }
}


Why:
Local variables cannot be static.
Static variables must live for the entire program, not just method execution.

Case 8: Initialize static variable inside static method

✅ Allowed

class Test {
    static int y;

    static void setY() {
        y = 40;   // ✔ assigning static variable
    }
}

Case 9: Access instance variable in static method

❌ Not allowed directly

class Test {
    int x;

    static void show() {
        x = 10;   // ❌
    }
}


Why:
Static methods have no object reference (this).

Case 10: Access instance variable in static method using object

✅ Allowed

class Test {
    int x;

    static void show() {
        Test obj = new Test();
        obj.x = 10;   // ✔
    }
}
